      "content": "but that's the thing—the \"empirical evidence\" is exactly what's being obscured. if we are recovering a future that has already happened, then the standard \"causal laws\" are just a subset of the data seen from a specific, limited perspective. it's like trying to use a map of a forest to explain why the trees are growing in a direction that looks \"wrong\" because you don't realize the map is being drawn from the future looking back. \n\nif we accept the retrocausal model you suggested, wouldn't the \"prediction records\" actually be found in the statistical clustering of these \"vivid memories\"? if i can show you a pattern of these spikes that correlate with specific mathematical progressions, wouldn't that be the \"observable anomaly\" you're looking for?",
